From 5efd1667db22594270a426eaba3140c15c074702 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 01:28:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/32] B70-QSA-D05: fused Triton select, native + select under
 capture, radix schedule by width, one-card bench

- B70_QSA_TOPK_SELECT_IMPL=triton (default torch): the native-anchored
  select in two launches instead of ~50 torch ops. _qsa_select_keep_kernel
  uses one program per row: it counts above the native k-th score (and the
  signed zeros), then makes a cumsum-ranked compaction pass in 1,024-column
  tiles bounded by the row's visible blocks. _qsa_select_order_kernel does
  tl.sort over the k kept int64 keys. Identical to the torch selector in the
  Triton interpreter on the tie-heavy suite. It has not been compiled on XPU
  yet; the next GPU window qualifies it.
- native_det + select under graph capture now runs the native op plus select,
  both device-only. Before, it took the radix threshold, ~60 small captured
  kernels per layer. B70_QSA_TOPK_CAPTURE=radix keeps the radix path; legacy
  still takes the torch path under capture.
- The radix pass schedule follows width: 4x8 bits up to 4,096 columns, else
  11/11/10, which matches persistent_topk's 2,048 decode bins.
  B70_QSA_TOPK_RADIX=8|11|16 forces a schedule; every schedule is tested.
- benchmarks/kernels/b70_qsa_tune/bench_topk_levers.py checks per-backend
  identity against torch and times each backend (wall time per call, launches
  included). It also times per-row against per-step decode select, with
  selection Jaccard, on one card.
